<Base title={title} description={description} updated="2026-11-22">
  <h1>How to get an apostille in Massachusetts</h1>
  <p class="lede">The Secretary of the Commonwealth, Public Records Division, Commissions Section, is the office in Massachusetts authorized to issue an apostille or certification for a document going to a foreign country. If the destination country is a signatory to the Hague Convention, the certificate is called an apostille.</p>

  <h2>At a glance</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Item</th><th>What the official page says</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Issuing office</td><td>William Francis Galvin, Secretary of the Commonwealth, Public Records Division, Commissions Section<Src href="https://www.sec.state.ma.us/divisions/commissions/apostilles.htm" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fee</td><td>$6.00 per document. Checks or money orders payable to the Commonwealth of Massachusetts. The official page and request form specify checks or money orders; the official brochure says cash is not recommended because the office cannot refund cash lost in transit. The request form says checks and money orders only.<Src href="https://www.sec.state.ma.us/divisions/commissions/apostilles.htm" /><Src href="https://www.sec.state.ma.us/divisions/commissions/download/authentication-form.pdf" /><Src href="https://www.sec.state.ma.us/divisions/commissions/download/apostille_and_certification_information.pdf" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mail</td><td>Yes. Include the destination country, payment, a prepaid return envelope, and the documents. For FedEx, DHL, or another express service, enclose a prepaid return envelope with a billing account number. The office does not accept credit cards as pre-payment for express mail.<Src href="https://www.sec.state.ma.us/divisions/commissions/apostilles.htm" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Boston office</td><td>Commissions Section, Room 1719, One Ashburton Place, Boston, MA 02108. Phone (617) 727-2836; <a href="mailto:comm@sec.state.ma.us">comm@sec.state.ma.us</a>. The apostilles page also lists 1-800-392-6090 and cis@sec.state.ma.us.<Src href="https://www.sec.state.ma.us/divisions/commissions/apostilles.htm" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Regional offices</td><td>Springfield: 436 Dwight Street, Room 102, Springfield, MA 01103, (413) 784-1376. Fall River: 218 South Main Street, Suite 206, Fall River, MA 02721, (508) 646-1374. The official page says number of documents and turnaround times at regional offices will vary.<Src href="https://www.sec.state.ma.us/divisions/commissions/apostilles.htm" /><Src href="https://www.sec.state.ma.us/divisions/commissions/download/authentication-form.pdf" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>In person (Boston)</td><td>Up to three documents can be done per customer at the counter. If you have four or more documents, you can pick them up after 3 p.m. on the next business day. Any document dropped off after 4 p.m. must be picked up the next business day. “There will be no exceptions.” The request form restates that more than three hand-delivered documents must be left overnight and picked up the next business day, no exceptions.<Src href="https://www.sec.state.ma.us/divisions/commissions/apostilles.htm" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mail processing time</td><td>Mailed requests are processed within two to three weeks.<Src href="https://www.sec.state.ma.us/divisions/commissions/apostilles.htm" /></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What can be certified</h2>
  <p>Certifications can only be obtained for documents with an original signature of a valid Massachusetts notary public, justice of the peace, city or town clerk or assistant, court clerk or assistant, or registrar of vital records. Photocopied signatures can’t be certified.<Src href="https://www.sec.state.ma.us/divisions/commissions/apostilles.htm" /></p>
  <p>The official brochure states that the Secretary’s office is the only office in Massachusetts authorized to issue an apostille or certification for a notarized document going to a foreign country, and that Massachusetts does not certify out-of-state documents; they must be certified by the state of origin.<Src href="https://www.sec.state.ma.us/divisions/commissions/download/apostille_and_certification_information.pdf" /></p>
  <p>The official request form adds that the office does not authenticate federal or out-of-state documents, and that it does not authenticate notarized photocopies of government-issued documents (its examples include a notarized photocopy of a Delaware certificate of good standing or of an FBI background check).<Src href="https://www.sec.state.ma.us/divisions/commissions/download/authentication-form.pdf" /></p>
  <p>Identify the destination country on the request. “Different countries require different types of certification. If the country is a signatory to the Hague Convention, the document will be called an apostille.”<Src href="https://www.sec.state.ma.us/divisions/commissions/apostilles.htm" /></p>

